This collection consists of a group of black and white photographs (from the late 19th century) showing black people in a variety of activities such as playing cricket, dancing, singing, attending a slave auction, standing at a railroad station in Louisiana, meeting friends at a ball, dancing under the stars in a New Orleans graveyard, dancing in front of a bonfire, gathering herbs for making medicinal folk remedies, attending church on Sunday mornings, listening to a female solo soprano at Union Square in New York City; serving as well as doing farm work; attending a funeral; walking down the aisle during the wedding ceremony; visiting friends; participating in community celebrations such as Mardi Gras; and playing games. Notably excluded are photographs of black entertainers such as minstrel shows.

